---
import '@trieur/core/trieur.css';
import DemoNav from '../../components/DemoNav.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ShortcutsDialog from '../../components/ShortcutsDialog.astro';
---

<Base title="Demo — throw, don't drop">
  <section class="wrap" style="padding-top:44px">
    <DemoNav />
    <h1 style="margin:0 0 8px">Throw, don't drop <span class="tag" style="vertical-align:middle">experimental</span></h1>
    <p style="color:var(--dim);max-width:70ch;margin:0 0 26px">
      A drop asks you to be accurate about a <em>coordinate</em>: let go over the right cell, or the card is filed in
      the wrong folder. A throw asks you to be accurate about a <em>direction</em>, which the hand is far better at. The
      card keeps the speed it had when you released it, travels a little further on its own, and lands where that
      carries it — so a distant zone no longer has to be reached, and two neighbouring cells no longer share a border
      you have to hit.
    </p>

    <div class="grid" style="margin-bottom:26px">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Where it comes to rest</h3>
        <p>
          The same arithmetic a scroll view uses: velocity decays exponentially at λ per millisecond, and the trip
          integrates to <code>v · λ/(1−λ)</code>. That fraction is a duration, which is why the knob is one — 170&nbsp;ms
          is λ ≈ 0.994, between iOS's fast scrolling and its normal one. A card is lighter than a scroll view.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The speed at lift-off</h3>
        <p>
          Not an average of the drag — a weighted quadratic fit of the last 100&nbsp;ms, which is what Android's
          <code>VelocityTracker</code> does. A fling is usually still accelerating when the finger leaves, and an
          average reports the speed of the gesture's middle instead of its end.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The model pulls</h3>
        <p>
          The iPhone keyboard's oldest trick: after <em>kno</em>, the <code>w</code> key's hit area grows while the key
          itself does not move a pixel. Here the tiles stay put and a confident suggestion quietly catches throws
          landing up to <b>bias × score</b> of a tile wide of it. The dashed circle in the debug view is that pull.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="demo demo-side">
      <div id="sorter"></div>
      <div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>Last release</h3>
          <dl id="read" class="kv">
            <div><dt>speed</dt><dd>—</dd></div>
            <div><dt>carried</dt><dd>—</dd></div>
            <div><dt>verdict</dt><dd>—</dd></div>
            <div><dt>filed into</dt><dd>—</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>Why a mosaic</h3>
          <p style="margin:0;color:var(--dim)">
            The zones here are deliberately small and adjacent — the case where dropping is a coin toss. A careful
            drag still drops exactly where you leave it; only a deliberate flick is projected, which is what the floor
            below 0.6&nbsp;px/ms is for.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:60px">
      <h2>Turning it on</h2>
      <pre><code>{`import { flick } from '@trieur/core/flick';

new Deck(el, {
  items, zones,
  layout: 'voronoi',
  plugins: [flick({
    ms: 170,       // how far ahead the release is projected
    decay: 0.994,  // the same thing as a deceleration rate, if you prefer
    min: 0.6,      // px/ms below which it is an ordinary drop
    bias: 0.4,     // how much wider the suggestion catches, in tiles
    debug: false,  // draw the vector while tuning
  })],
});`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        It is a <a href="/docs/plugins/">plugin</a>, not an option: the projection, the velocity fit and the debug
        renderer are about a kilobyte gzipped that a page which never throws a card should not be carrying. The deck
        knows nothing about any of it — it asks whoever is listening where the gesture is pointing, and the first
        answer wins.
      </p>
      <p>
        A throw resolves to the <b>nearest tile</b>, not to the region under the landing point: the landing point is a
        prediction, not a touch, and past the edge of the stage there are no regions left to be in. "The nearest tile in
        the direction you threw" always has an answer — and a zone the drag was heading <em>away</em> from is never one
        of the candidates, whatever the arithmetic says.
      </p>
      <p>
        The projection is capped at the stage diagonal. Past the edge, every extra pixel of reach aims at the same zone
        anyway, and an uncapped throw off a fast trackpad lands in another postcode. Velocity is sampled on the raw
        pointer moves rather than the throttled ones — a flick is over in three frames, and averaging across a frame
        boundary flattens precisely the peak that made it a flick — and a finger that rests before letting go clears the
        window: a gesture that stopped has thrown nothing, however fast it arrived.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is marked experimental because the right numbers depend on your zones: a dock of six columns wants little
        reach, a mosaic of twenty cells wants a lot. The settings panel has all four on sliders, and the debug view
        draws what they do.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <ShortcutsDialog />
</Base>

<script>
  import { Deck } from '@trieur/core';
  import { flick } from '@trieur/core/flick';
  import { createRecommender } from '@trieur/learn';
  import { meta, shuffled, zones, type Link } from '../../data/links';
  import { renderCard } from '../../scripts/card';
  import { deckGui } from '../../scripts/gui';
  import { shortcutsButton } from '../../scripts/shortcuts';

  const $ = (s: string) => document.querySelector(s)!;
  const cells = $('#read').querySelectorAll('dd');

  const deck = new Deck<Link>($('#sorter') as HTMLElement, {
    // live on touch straight away: this is a demo, and the play button is what it is
    // demonstrating everywhere else
    touchPreview: false,
    items: shuffled(24),
    zones,
    meta,
    renderCard,
    layout: 'voronoi',
    plugins: [flick({ debug: true })],
    advisor: createRecommender({ key: 'demo-flick' }),
    onSort: (_item, zone) => {
      cells[3]!.textContent = zone.id;
    },
  });

  // the plugin reports every release it looked at, thrown or dropped
  deck.root.addEventListener('trieur:throw', (e) => {
    const { speed, carried, thrown } = (e as CustomEvent).detail;
    cells[0]!.textContent = `${speed.toFixed(2)} px/ms`;
    cells[1]!.textContent = thrown ? `${Math.round(carried)} px` : '—';
    cells[2]!.textContent = thrown ? 'throw' : 'drop';
  });

  deckGui(deck);
  shortcutsButton(deck);
  deck.focus();
</script>
